From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] ste: set NFS timestamps the way this file already does

PutNFSProperties() populates a []syscall.Timeval with a composite literal
whose Sec/Usec fields are int64 values. That only compiles on 64-bit Linux
targets: syscall.Timeval's fields are int64 on linux/amd64 and linux/arm64
but int32 on linux/386, linux/arm and linux/mips, so GOARCH=arm fails with

  ste/downloader-azureFiles_linux.go:255:9: cannot use lastModifiedTimeSec
      (variable of type int64) as int32 value in struct literal
  ste/downloader-azureFiles_linux.go:255:36: cannot use
      int64(lastModifiedTimeNsec / 1000) (value of type int64) as int32
      value in struct literal
  (and the same two for line 256)

The fix is to do what setDates() already does earlier in this same file for
the SMB equivalent of the same operation: build a unix.Timespec and
call unix.UtimesNanoAt(). golang.org/x/sys/unix is already imported here for
exactly that. syscall is not needed for anything else in the file, so its
import goes too.

unix.TimeToTimespec() is used rather than the unix.NsecToTimespec() that
setDates() uses, because it is strictly safer and costs nothing: it builds
from the same t.Unix()/t.Nanosecond() pair the removed code used, and returns
ERANGE if the seconds do not fit the target's field width instead of silently
wrapping. On 32-bit that field is int32, so this is a live case (see Caveat).

Provenance
----------
Origin:       Not upstream. Written for this repo against
              Azure/azure-storage-azcopy v10.32.7.
Author:       Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Upstream:     NO -- not submitted. Microsoft ships AzCopy for linux/amd64
              and linux/arm64 only (the v10.32.7 release has no linux/arm
              or linux/386 asset), so upstream has no CI leg that would
              have caught this and no declared support for the target this
              image builds for. Worth sending, but "we built it for an
              architecture upstream does not claim to support" is the
              honest status until it is.
Disposition:  BUILD BLOCKER. Without it `make azcopy` cannot produce an
              ARMv7 binary at all -- this is a compile error, not a
              warning, so the failure is loud and total.
Behaviour:    THIS IS NOT A PURE REFACTOR, and an earlier revision of this
              header wrongly claimed it was. Three real differences, all of
              them on 64-bit targets too, all of them improvements:
                1. Resolution. utimensat takes nanoseconds; utimes took
                   microseconds. The removed code truncated the sub-second
                   part to microseconds. Azure Files timestamps carry 100 ns
                   resolution, so this now preserves what it is given.
                   (Note it is NOT the case that syscall.NsecToTimeval
                   would have been equivalent-but-arch-safe: that helper
                   does `nsec += 999` and rounds UP to the next microsecond,
                   so it would have shifted every non-exact timestamp
                   forward. That is why this patch does not use it.)
                2. Out-of-range times now fail loudly. TimeToTimespec
                   returns ERANGE where the old code silently truncated
                   int64 seconds into whatever the field held.
                3. The syscall changes from utimes(2) to utimensat(2). Both
                   have been in Linux since 2.6.22; utimensat is what the
                   SMB path in this same file has always used.
Caveat:       On a 32-bit target Timespec.Sec is int32, so Azure Files NFS
              last-write-times beyond 2038 (or before 1902) cannot be
              represented. That limit is the platform's, not this patch's --
              but where the original would have wrapped silently, this now
              returns an ERANGE error naming the timestamp. Called out so
              the next reader does not have to rediscover it.

Verified:     builds clean for GOOS=linux GOARCH=arm GOARM=7, GOARCH=386 and
              GOARCH=amd64 with go 1.26.3 (the version Buildroot 2026.05.1
              pins in package/go/go.mk); `go vet ./ste/` reports nothing new.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
---
 ste/downloader-azureFiles_linux.go | 27 ++++++++++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ste/downloader-azureFiles_linux.go b/ste/downloader-azureFiles_linux.go
index cf86e54..1fb70b4 100644
--- a/ste/downloader-azureFiles_linux.go
+++ b/ste/downloader-azureFiles_linux.go
@@ -11,7 +11,6 @@ import (
 	"strconv"
 	"strings"
 	"sync"
-	"syscall"
 
 	"github.com/Azure/azure-sdk-for-go/sdk/storage/azfile/file"
 
@@ -245,19 +244,21 @@ func (*azureFilesDownloader) PutNFSProperties(sip INFSPropertyBearingSourceInfoP
 
 	lastWriteTime := propHolder.FileLastWriteTime()
 
-	// Convert the time to Unix timestamp (seconds and nanoseconds)
-	lastModifiedTimeSec := lastWriteTime.Unix()        // Seconds part
-	lastModifiedTimeNsec := lastWriteTime.Nanosecond() // Nanoseconds part
-
-	// Convert the time to syscall.Timeval type (seconds and microseconds)
-	// syscall.Timeval expects seconds and microseconds, so we convert the nanoseconds
-	tv := []syscall.Timeval{
-		{Sec: lastModifiedTimeSec, Usec: int64(lastModifiedTimeNsec / 1000)}, // Convert nanoseconds to microseconds
-		{Sec: lastModifiedTimeSec, Usec: int64(lastModifiedTimeNsec / 1000)}, // Set both atime and mtime to the same timestamp
+	// Convert the time to a unix.Timespec, which setDates() above already uses
+	// for the SMB equivalent of this operation. A syscall.Timeval composite
+	// literal cannot be used here: its Sec and Usec fields are int64 on 64-bit
+	// Linux but int32 on linux/386, linux/arm and linux/mips, so populating
+	// them from int64 values is a compile error on every 32-bit target.
+	// unix.TimeToTimespec builds the architecture-correct struct from the same
+	// Unix()/Nanosecond() pair, and returns ERANGE when the seconds do not fit
+	// the target's field width instead of silently wrapping.
+	ts, err := unix.TimeToTimespec(lastWriteTime)
+	if err != nil {
+		return fmt.Errorf("Failed to represent lastModifiedTime %v for %s. Error: %w", lastWriteTime, txInfo.Destination, err)
 	}
 
-	// Use syscall.Utimes to set modification times
-	err = syscall.Utimes(txInfo.Destination, tv)
+	// Set both atime and mtime to the same timestamp
+	err = unix.UtimesNanoAt(unix.AT_FDCWD, txInfo.Destination, []unix.Timespec{ts, ts}, 0 /* flags */)
 	if err != nil {
 		return fmt.Errorf("Failed to set lastModifiedTime for %s. Error: %w", txInfo.Destination, err)
 	}
-- 
